<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monckton Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Monckton Commission was a British advisory commission chaired by Lord Monckton that reviewed the future of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> in 1960. Its report found deep African hostility to the federation and recommended wider African political representation and that member territories should be able to leave. It encouraged the secession of Nyasaland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, and the federation was dissolved at the end of 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monckton-commission.jpg" alt="Monckton Commission" width="300" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Beneaththelandslide at English Wikipedia. · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federation_of_Rhodesia_and_Nyasaland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The commission's official name was the Advisory Commission for the Review of the Constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a>. The British government set it up under Walter Monckton, 1st Viscount Monckton of Brenchley, to make proposals for the future of the Federation, also called the Central African Federation, which joined Southern Rhodesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> and Nyasaland, the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>. It had 20 members appointed to represent six interest groups. It toured the Federation in February 1960 with limited terms of reference, and was boycotted by the opposition Labour Party and by the black nationalists of Nyasaland and Northern Rhodesia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Released in October 1960, the report found widespread and sincere opposition to the Federation in the two northern territories and concluded that it could not be kept except by force or by massive changes in racial legislation. It proposed black African majorities in the legislatures of Nyasaland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, more voting rights for black Africans, less racial discrimination and the option for these territories to leave after five years. Because the commission seemed weighted towards keeping the Federation, its report disappointed the British government.</p><p>The Federal Prime Minister, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roy-welensky">Roy Welensky</a>, called the report the death knell of federation and rejected it. Black nationalists opposed it on other grounds, since they wanted an end to federation and independence under black majority rule. The British government broadly accepted the report. Over the objections of the Governor of Nyasaland, Sir Robert Armitage, Colonial Secretary Iain Macleod released <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> from detention on 1 April 1960 and opened talks with him on Nyasaland's constitutional future.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A Federal Review Conference, provided for in the act of 1953 that created the Federation, opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 5 December 1960. Banda walked out on 12 December, and the federal talks were suspended on 16 December. The conference mainly made clear the views of Banda for Nyasaland and Kaunda for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> that their peoples wanted to leave the Federation, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joshua-nkomo">Joshua Nkomo</a> that the African majority of Southern Rhodesia wanted majority rule. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi-congress-party">Malawi Congress Party</a>'s victory in the elections of August 1961, Nyasaland became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> on 6 July 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monckton Commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Monckton Commission</b> concluded in 1960 that the Federation could be kept only by force or by massive changes in racial legislation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1959 emergency in Nyasaland and the Devlin and <b>Monckton</b> reports undermined its legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a></li></ul></section>
